From 34feec287010e79849a0d1d7fffddaeb0117e0df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 08:35:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 38/88] Backport: fix protocol input validation (upstream
 07512b109)

From the stable branch after 1.14.2, without its dependency updates and
without the QUIC sniffer part, which "sniff: bound the CRYPTO frames of a
QUIC Initial" already covers. What it fixes on the paths podkop uses:

- ja3: a supported_versions or signature_algorithms list longer than its
  extension was read past the end: one QUIC Initial from any client in the
  LAN, sniffed, crashed the process (test added);
- the websocket and HTTP upgrade clients read the bytes that came with the
  101 response into a buffer of length 0, dropping them; the websocket client
  also deleted Sec-WebSocket-Protocol from the configured headers at its
  first dial, so later dials went without it;
- a websocket control frame is bounded by the payload size of the protocol;
- gRPC lite keeps a frame length past 2 GiB from turning negative;
- a UDP sniff that resumed with packets read earlier returned them again,
  and the caller appended them a second time;
- fakeip refuses a domain longer than 255 bytes;
- the trojan mux and the server side of naive, HTTP and HTTP upgrade keep
  the bytes buffered with the request, and release buffers on errors.

The same commit's checks for tailscale, bridge, openconnect, openvpn and the
shadowsocks multi-user server come along unchanged.
